            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the POSITION of the largest value in a tensor - its index, not the value itself.\n\nUse it to pick a winner: which sensor reads highest, which frequency band is loudest, which class a set of scores votes for, where the brightest pixel is. If several cells share the largest value, the first one is given. There is no t.argmin: use t.argmax on the tensor times -1, or t.min with a dim for the indices.\n\nTHE INDEX WITH NO dim:\nWith no argument the answer is ONE whole number counting the cells of the whole tensor in reading order - along row 0, then row 1, and so on - not a row and a column. In a tensor 8 columns wide, index 21 is row 2, column 5: the row is the index divided by the width (whole-number division, // 8) and the column is the remainder (% 8).\n\nTHE dim ARGUMENT:\nWith no argument the WHOLE tensor is reduced to one value. A number typed after the name (t.argmax 0) chooses a dim to reduce along and adds a dim inlet to the node - it is the argument that creates them, so a node made without one cannot be given a dim later (retype it). For a table of rows and columns, dim 0 reduces DOWN each column (one answer per column) and dim 1 ALONG each row (one answer per row). The dim named is the one that disappears: a 6 by 8 tensor gives 8 answers on dim 0 and 6 on dim 1. Negative numbers count from the end: -1 is the last dim. With a dim each\nanswer is a position along THAT dim: dim 0 gives, for each column, the row it was found in; dim 1 gives, for each row, the column.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.argmax <dim: int>\nDefault: no dim - one index into the whole tensor.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.argmax 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every one that arrives is searched and the answer sent - this is the only inlet that makes the node send. A list or a NumPy array is first turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu; a tensor is used as it is, on its own device, and the answer stays there. An empty tensor is ignored: nothing is sent.\nA NaN counts as the largest: the index of the first NaN is given.\n\ndim (inlet, only when an argument was given):\nA whole number. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next tensor uses it. A dim the tensor does not have (2 or more for a table, or 1 for a 1-D tensor) is an error: the console prints \"Dimension out of range\" and nothing is sent.\nThere is no keep_dims option on this node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmax index:\nWhole numbers (int64). With no dim, one index (a 0-dimensional tensor);\nwith a dim, a tensor of indices, one per column (dim 0) or row (dim 1).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1, written in the cells; click its button for new ones. The plain t.argmax gives the index of the largest of all 48; // 8 and % 8 split it into the row and column - find that cell in the picture. The slider sets the dim of the second node through 't bang int' (number first, then a bang that re-sends the held tensor). Its list holds, at dim 0, the row of each column's largest value (8 numbers); at dim 1, the column of each row's (6 numbers).\n\nRELATED:\nt.max gives the largest value, and with a dim the same indices too. t.argsort ranks every value, not just the top one. t.argwhere lists every position where a condition holds. np.argmax is the NumPy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
